      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet, U10 pin 2 (DIAG_EN) is connected to +12V, while pin 1 (IN) is connected to NET0010.",
      "expected": "DIAG_EN and IN should both be driven by the ESP32 GPIO1 heater-control net, as specified.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "U10 TPS1H200AQDGNRQ1: pin 2 DIAG_EN connects to +12V; pin 1 IN connects to NET0010 (Microcontroller sheet, page 2)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "IN/DIAG_EN (pins 1/2) are both driven from ESP32 GPIO1 on the /Microcontroller/HEATER net."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "DIAG_EN is no longer controlled by the heater signal. Tying this control input directly to 12 V may exceed its allowed input voltage and damage U10; even if it survives, its diagnostic enable state will not track the intended GPIO control.",
      "confidence": 0.98
    }
  ],
  "summary": "U10's DIAG_EN pin is tied to +12V in the schematic instead of the GPIO1 heater-control net specified for both DIAG_EN and IN. This is a clear control-net deviation and may overvoltage the switch input."
}

